### 3\. Example of using both in Ubuntu

Terminal 1 – Discover devices and services on your own network:

bash
nmap -sV 192.168.1.1


Terminal 2 – Capture network traffic:

bash
sudo tcpdump -i any -w network.pcap


Terminal 3 – Analyse the captured traffic:

bash
tshark -r network.pcap


You can also open network.pcap in Wireshark.

### 4\. My recommended cybersecurity toolkit

| Tool       | Main purpose                   |
| ---------- | ------------------------------ |
| Nmap       | Network scanning               |
| Wireshark  | Packet analysis                |
| TShark     | Command-line packet analysis   |
| Tcpdump    | Packet capture                 |
| Burp Suite | Web request interception       |
| Ettercap   | LAN interception testing       |
| Bettercap  | Network security testing       |
| Scapy      | Packet manipulation            |
| Metasploit | Authorized penetration testing |

My advice: Don't abandon Nmap because you have Wireshark. If you want to understand network security properly, learn both.
